argin": -9.5, "poll_sd": 12.4, "poll_weight": 0.16, "fundamentals": {"margin": -2.05, "lean": -6.0, "environment": 3.95, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": -13.67, "sigma_race": 5.8, "sigma_total": 6.18, "baseline_margin": -9.1, "analyst_adjustment": -0.4, "key_factors": ["South Carolina's strong Republican lean (R+8 partisan baseline) in an open-seat race", "All six expert ratings put the race at Likely R or better, with several calling it Solid/Safe R", "Prediction markets price Johnson's chances at only about 5 percent", "Both available polls, though Democratic-sponsored and modest in quality, show Wilson ahead by 8 points"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point firmly Republican: an R+8 state, an open seat with no incumbency offset, and a favorable national environment for the GOP. Polling averages roughly 9 points for Wilson, markets give Johnson about 5 percent, and every major rater places the race at Likely R or safer, so the forecast sits near a 9-10 point Republican edge.", "watch": "A strong debate performance by Johnson or a shift in the national environment toward Democrats could tighten the race, but he would need a substantial polling move to make it competitive.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Henry McMaster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's governorship is open with Republican Henry McMaster term-limited, and Attorney General Alan Wilson enters the general election as the clear favorite over Democratic state Representative Jermaine Johnson. The state's Republican lean of about 8 points gives Wilson a solid structural advantage, and the political environment in 2026 also tilts toward the GOP.\n\nAvailable polling, though limited and from Democratic-leaning sponsors, shows Wilson ahead by 8 points, and prediction markets give Johnson only about a 5 percent chance of winning. Expert raters uniformly place the race at Likely R or safer. Johnson has drawn attention for his primary upset and faith-